most bioinformatic analyses  are going to be considered as  pre-clinical  that means it is based on data generated  in lab experiments or retrospective  studies  to explore these let's look at examples  of cell lines derived from patient  tumors in oncology  animal models of human disease  and explore how these can be used  alongside databases on various specific  diseases for example tcga and oncology  then we will discuss how such insights  can be validated and what considerations  they bring to biobanking and clinical  trials next  technologies and experiments are already  affecting research hospitals and are  used by pharmaceutical industry to  design specific treatments and adapt  them to the needs of individual patients  a lot of times  not all of these experiments are  possible on real patients instead  we can select tumors in different types  of patients  produce those tumors as immortalized  cell lines and use those cell lines to  understand the basic biology  unfortunately that biology cannot be  fully studied just using cell lines  because cell lines don't have all of the  elements of the system or the whole body  that actually functions as a whole  and so in many cases in oncology instead  of just using cell lines there are also  tumors that could be extracted in full  from a patient  grafted into an animal model  and then studied in terms of their  interaction with the rest of the body  ultimately such experiments  can provide better insights into  biological factors that are relevant for  patients  and different types of tests could be  first performed on these models  before we actually develop a clinical  trial where such treatments or  diagnostic procedures are used on actual  patients this is also changing the way  that we apply big data analysis  techniques or bioinformatics in real  world clinical needs so typical process  is to collect integrate and analyze big  data coming from a variety of sources  and ultimately change the way that this  can be used in a big data approach for  precision medicine we can see this  oftentimes used when large scale data is  being collected on a particular disease  a good example is the cancer genome  atlas the cancer genome atlas is a work  performed over several decades to  collect samples with multi-omic data on  patients and use their clinical  phenotypes to find important  associations the cancer genome data is  available through the genome data  commons portal that allows us to find  multi-omics datasets with clinical  information about each sample which  gives a much better understanding of how  cancer variation is  diagnosed and treated and how successful  those treatments can be these  large-scale data sets are continuing to  be collected and today we have the all  of us data set as well the all of us  research program which is led by the  national institutes of health is  building one of the largest biomedical  data resources of its kind the all of us  research hub stores health data from a  diverse group of participants from  across the united states researchers can  access all of us data and tools to  conduct studies to help improve our  understanding of human health  now it also provides us with a much  better understanding of what we call  healthy or the variation of healthy  individuals that can be used as a  reference when we study subgroups of  patients or patients that are of  different ages  sexes  ethnic backgrounds  and socio-economic status in addition  there are projects that are aimed at  characterizing the healthy condition  and pre-clinical variation one such  example is the nih human microbiome  project that allows us to look at  different types of microbiome  perturbation that is associated with  different types of diseases  these include depression  autism spectrum disorder  cardiovascular disease  asthma arthritis cancer and diabetes  by understanding the microbiome  diversity composition and changes  associated with different types of  phenotypes we can start seeing the  impact of the microbiome on diagnostics  but also treatment because patients with  different microbiome can metabolize  treatment at different rates  now that we have reviewed the rationale  for bioinformatics and ngs in biomedical  research in the clinic let's review case  studies of clinical research with real  data these include oncology  neurodegenerative diseases like  alzheimer's disease and infectious  diseases like covet 19 malaria or ebola  we can look at how viral genomics and  evolution can help us understand the  spread of a disease and identify  variants of concern in pathogens  or  genetic predisposition of some  populations to be more susceptible to  acquire disease and potentially  have worse outcomes we can look at  immune responses where we characterize  gene expression and study biomarkers or  genes that are associated with a  particular response and sometimes we can  look at microbiome and see how  microbiome plays a role in disease  progression drug metabolism and also  predisposes certain people to different  clinical risks on the onyx logic portal  you will find a number of case studies  and curated data sets around these  different topics some of them are based  on full analysis of data sets that are  already published  simply by following the steps described  in these case studies you will be able  to make important discoveries that today  are being used in the clinic some of  them have to do with drug metabolism  identification of somatic mutations in  tumor development  inherited or germline mutations like the  sickle cell anemia rare genetic diseases  as well as drug resistance associated  with different types of antibiotics and  pathogens oftentimes just by reading the  abstract you get a good grasp of how  bioinformaticians can use such data to  model precision medicine for example on  this project modeling precision medicine  of breast cancer you will find how  researchers use machine learning to  study transcriptomic data and identify  subgroups of individuals  that are going to respond differently to  different treatments in the study  researchers use multi-omics data  integration to look at different types  of molecular profiles  integrate them and compare them to  reference databases like the tcga to  understand response to treatment to  drugs that are either already used in  the clinic or being studied in clinical  trials to continue learning we invite  you to explore these associated  resources for the lecture  we will go over the coursework the case  studies and other examples from  literature  to benefit from today's lecture you can  select one of these to refresh your  memory of basic terminology  learn more about the various techniques  or practice what we have learned with  curated data sets on the topics that we  covered in today's session  the contents of the session correspond  with practical lessons that you can  complete independently that are  available on the onyx logic learn portal  with the full course specific lessons  that are of relevance to today's session  include  lesson two bioinformatics in healthcare  from course one introduction to  bioinformatics  in this lesson you will get an overview  of examples from healthcare area where  bioinformatics can be applied you will  also see examples and videos of medical  professionals discussing bioinformatics  and precision medicine applications  another good course to look at is bytes  and molecules  this course is a great place for those  who was confused by a lot of the terms  that we used in today's lecture anyone  that would like to review the basic  terminology and get a good overview of  topics that we cover in this course  should look at this course in full  but specifically we looked at this  lesson  lesson six called introduction to  genomics if you are interested in  genomics you can also look at these  additional lessons in the course three  course called genomics in lesson two you  will see how you can work with dna  sequences in r  and in lesson three you will get an  introduction to large-scale genomics the  full course contains a lot more  information that you can choose to  explore independently some of these will  be covered in the future lectures but do  not worry about completing them for now  at the end you will also find examples  that i already mentioned  these examples include such projects  like ebola  immune response changes in cancer  modeling cancer precision medicine  or tuberculosis infection and treatment  finding drug resistance variants  so let's recap what we have learned so  far  first  we learned how combining clinical and  molecular data helps reveal underlying  factors contributing to the disease  which further helps us provide a precise  diagnosis and effective treatment to the  patient  further we learned about next-generation  sequencing technologies for genomic  transcriptomic and metagenomic data that  provide important insights for precision  medicine applications  we also discussed the role of  bioinformatics in taking research  results from the lab to the clinic to  benefit patient treatment and diagnosis  finally we introduced a set of case  studies in precision medicine from  inherited sickle cell anemia to drug  resistance and mycobacterium  tuberculosis  in the next session session two on  genomic data analysis we will go one  step further to better understand what  is genomic data  how do you perform variant calling  analysis  what is the difference between germline  and somatic mutations  and how to determine a variance clinical  relevance  at the end of session 2  you will have a thorough understanding  of genomic data  variant calling analysis as well as  specific techniques for clinical  annotation of variants in cancer  [Music]  you

